Hi

I converted the root-filesystem from ufs to advfs as follows:
the harddrive has four partitions: a, b, g, and h
a ist for root, b is swap and the others are /usr and /tmp

1.   vdump -0f /tmp/root-bck /                                 (storing
of root-filesystem on partition h)
2.   reboot the workstation from installation CDROM and start in
single-mode
3.   cd /dev
4.   MAKEDEV rz16
(make disk-partitions visible)
5.   mkfdmn -r -t rz16 /dev/rz16a root_domain
6.   mkfset root_domain root                                     (create
file domain and fileset)
7.   mount -t advfs root_domain#root /mnt
8.   mount /dev/rz16h /tmp
(mounting partitions a and h for restore)
9.   vrestore -xf /tmp/root-bck -D /mnt             (restoring
root-filesystem)
10. umount /tmp
(unmounting partition h)
11. disklabel -r /dev/rrz16a > /tmp/rz16label
12. disklabel -t advfs -r -R /dev/rrz16a /tmp/rz16label rz16     (making
partition a bootable)
13. replace '/dev/rz16a  /  ufs  rw  11' with 'root_domain#root  /
advfs  rw  1 1' in fstab
14. reboot

If I reboot, the system starts up booting and comes up, till the
root-filesystem is mounted,
then the following message appears: 'mounting root_domain#root on /:not
enough space'
then the system comes up in single mode. If I invoke 'mount', I see,
that the root-filesystem
is only mounted as read only not as read/write.
I replaced also the order of the entries in fstab without success.
There cannot be too few space on partition a. I tested this with df and
saw that only 22% is
used., so there must be too few memory space?
If I convert the filesystem back to ufs with the same method, the system
boots up and runs normally.

Please tell me, what the problem could be?
May I have forgotten to enter or change an entry in another
configuration file?

Thank you very much.

Tom


