In article <35BD4E61.7611@bhp.com.au>, Kelvin Lynch <lynch.kelvin.k@bhp.com.au> writes:
:I have heard a notion that it might possible to boot an Alpha using a
:floppy disk....not just for firmware updates or similar, but actually
:bringing the system up to something approximating single user mode 
:
:Is this possible ?

  Yes, it is theoretically possible, but enough of the operating system
  kernel would have to fit on the device or there would have to be some
  floppy-swapping logic in the bootstrap, and I am not aware of any
  interactive Alpha operating system environment -- interactive meaning
  beyond the limited capabilities of the firmware loader and similar such
  environments -- that is small enough to fit on a floppy, or set up to
  swap floppies.

  I could certainly believe that someone could code a dedicated (limited)
  operating system environment that could boot off the CD-ROM -- such as
  an application environment for an embedded system...

  But most operating systems and environments typical expect a local or
  served CD-ROM drive is available...

  The pain around the creation, support, and use of the multiple-floppy
  environment with certain OpenVMS VAX systems and the standalone BACKUP
  kit is something I remember and would not wish to repeat... :-)

:Whilst I am not particularly well versed in the OSF startup sequence, I
:imagine that the bootstrap and the kernel (osf_boot and vmunix) both
:need to be available for the boot to succeed... and given a kernel size
:approaching 10 Mb, a floppy disk wouldn't have the capacity.

  Well, I think you're a few megabytes short of a bootstrap.  :-)

