> In article <35BD4E61.7611@bhp.com.au>, Kelvin Lynch <lynch.kelvin.k@bhp.com.au> writes:
> :I have heard a notion that it might possible to boot an Alpha using a
> :floppy disk....not just for firmware updates or similar, but actually
> :bringing the system up to something approximating single user mode 
> :
> :Is this possible ?

This may not be what you're looking for, but Linux can be booted from
floppy.  If booting from ARC or AlphaBIOS, you use two floppies.  The
first one holds the loader (MILO), and the second holds the kernel.
If using SRM it is possible to boot with just the one floppy holding
the kernel.

Once you have the kernel, you can mount a root, which might be another
floppy.  You also have the capability to load a root filesystem from a
floppy into a ramdisk, which leaves the floppy drive free.

So, the normal procedure is to use three floppies.  Loader, kernel,
root.  This gives you a very minimal system, but enough to make
repairs or get started on an install.

Matt Grossman
