Someone at Netscape told me that the java problem depended on the
hardware model, not versions of run-time libraries or whatever.  I
have the same problem on an AlphaStation 200.  Anyway, that was one
that was specifically mentioned as having problems.  For what it's
worth they said that Java will eventually come up if you wait like 30
minutes or so.  Gee, thanks.

But Netscape's claims of "hardware problems" are quite empty (as I
told them).  Java runs fine on Netscape 3.01 and the same hardware.
There's nothing that it HAS to do that it can't do some other way.
But they don't seem particularly committed to the Alpha platform...

What really bugs me though is DEC's treatment of this (maybe Compaq
will do better -- ha).  If they really want DUnix workstations to
succeed, they should have busted a gut to make sure popular software
like Netscape was running properly.  Even to the point of loaning
technical experts to Netscape.

I won't buy another DEC for a desktop machine again.  It's simply too
aggravating.  We can use an Alpha as a compute server, but my next
desktop machine will be an Intel box running Linux.  Thanks DEC for
running me off.....

