Kevin Maguire wrote:

> And doesn't seem to fix the problems with Java that have been bugging
> it since V4 was released.  I downloaded and tried 4.05 just now, once
> again choose a simple web page with some java and the whole things
> just hangs.  I'm using DUnix 4.0D, AFAIK I have all the latest
> versions of run-time libraries, etc ..... ??

Actually, your "up to dateness" is your problem. Netscape is still building using an extremely old version of Digital UNIX (actually,
they're still on DEC OSF/1, before the name change), and that's a big part of the reason that it doesn't work well with the new
system. In particular, they're using the old Xt library, prior to the change from the old select()-based input to the new (standard)
poll()-based input. They depended on the select() mechanism, and break with the new Xt library.

I've been using Communicator 4.05 successfully on 4.0D, and even on pre-field test variants of 5.0, with a number of "fix-ups". For
one thing, you need to use the resurrected old Xt library, which you can find in /usr/lib/netscape. Even if you're using the new
Communicator, you should be pointing its LD_LIBRARY_PATH at a directory with that libXt.so. (I've seen some evidence that you do NOT
want to point at the kit's /usr/lib/netscape, because it has other files specific to older Netscape code that may interfere with the
new Netscape.) I install into /usr/local/lib/communicator, and create a shlib directory there, and run netscape with the following
script (which I call "netcomm" to avoid confusion:)

     #!/usr/bin/ksh
     set -e
     export MOZILLA_HOME=/usr/local/lib/communicator/
     ## export MOZILLA_NO_ASYNC_DNS=True
     export NPX_PLUGIN_PATH=/usr/local/lib/communicator/plugins
     export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/usr/local/lib/communicator/shlib
     exec /usr/local/lib/communicator/netscape $*

Note that while I had used MOZILLA_NO_ASYNC_DNS for 4.01, I've found that things seem to work more smoothly (most of the time) without
it on 4.05. (Although, in the past few days, I've started getting a lot of DNS lookup failures, and I may end up turning async DNS off
again.)

I also frequently experienced the "prolonged first time Java hang" with 4.01, though apparently it does eventually clear, if you're
patient. (I don't think I ever waited.) At some point, that problem simply "went away" for me, and I've never seen it with 4.05.
(Which may or may not mean anything.)

