In article <6pf4sj$460$1@Starbase.NeoSoft.COM>,
Cameron Laird <claird@Starbase.NeoSoft.COM> wrote:
> I'm looking for advice.  What should my next step be?
> 
> I have a stack of sheets with LICENSE PAK in front of me.
> I'm looking at an AlphaServer 1000A 5/333 with DU 4.0B
> Rev 564.  After an hour of fumbling, `lmf list` gives me
> 
>   Product                   Status                     Users: Total      Active
> 
>   OSF-SVR                   enabled   
>   OSF-BASE                  enabled
>   OSF-USR                   active                            4          0
>   OSF-USR                   active, multiple                  4          0     
> 
> Any attempt to telnet with a user other than root returns
> "Too many users logged on already."

Cameron-

You need to do:

	lmf list full for OSF-USR

Look at the ``Number of units'' and ``Activity Table Code'' fields for each
of the licenses that you'll see.  Number of units is how many license units
that particular license grants.  Activity Table Code of `CONSTANT=100' (which
is what you'll probably see) indicates that each interactive user of that
type of license requires that many units.

If one of your two licenses grants a ``Number of units'' of 0, try deleting
the *other* license:

	lmf delete OSF-USR DEC <authorization number>

> Do I need a NET-APP-SUP-200?

No you do not.  NAS200 gives you the right to install the NAS package.
Installing that and running `nasinstall' will give you some additional
licenses, notably DECNSR-NET-SVR (the NetWorker full server license) and
ADVFS-UTILITIES, but you do not need NAS200 to run.  In fact, Digital has
retired the NAS stuff altogether.  As far as I know there is no expiration
on the licenses that NAS grants, so you can theoretically continue to use
the NAS-granted licenses, but this also means that you better keep an old
copy of the SPL CDs, because none of the NAS stuff is on any of the recent
CDs.


> and INT-ELEC-LOCKER-ADMIN?

I don't know what that is, but it's not required for interactive login use.

Tim
