> I have been asked to detail the solution, so:
> 
> Digital came back to me with the magic formula (SCSI bus * 8 + SCSI ID No)
> Bus=1
> ID=4
> (1*8+4=12)
> Giving tz12.
> 
> It seems to work, despite there being no entry for tz89 in the config file.
> Doing a "file" indicates that it is a tzxx, but it seems to get the info
> from the device itself.
> Did a vdump, & it works OK, I will have to say I'm very
> impressed with Digital & their Kit.
> 
> Terry
> 

Typically, when you do your ./MAKEDEV, you only need to specify the
device type (t = tape), interface (z = SCSI) and the ID number (in your
case, 12). MAKEDEV will then create several device special files, BUT
they will have names like rmt0a or nrmt0a. 

Specifying the model number is only needed when doing a newfs. However,
a newfs is not needed on tape drives (usually... There are probably
exceptions I don't know about). They are accessed directly (read stream)
by whatever archive/backup program you use. Have you noticed that
mounting of a tape is not neccessary? (actually... Is it possible?)

Since you seem to have everything working, I won't go into anymore
details.

DEC... Err.. Compaq/DEC is usually pretty good when you need help out of
a jam (I've had them here many a time replacing disks, tapes, processor,
whatenot). Hope it stays that way (ha!).

PS - I came across the same difficulty finding out the SCSI ID numbers
when multiple SCSI cards are installed. The equation is buried pretty
deep in the documentation in an unrelated topic. You would think in the
MAKEDEV man pages, or there abouts in the documentation, would be the
perfect place for it. Go figure...

