In article <35B3BC60.337C@auracom.com>  writes:
> Hi:
> 
> I'm running an AlphaStation 400 4/233 and Digital Unix 3.2.
> 
> A user on a network-connected Sun Sparc has been processing
> files stored on an Alpha disk via an NFS mount. Unpredictably, about
> once or twice per week the Alpha simply stops its NFS responding as pwer
> the message on the sun, 
> 
> 	nfs mount: alpha400: NFS Service not responding.
> 
> Once both machines are rebooted, we're ok(for awhile).
> 
> Question 1) Any suggestions as to why this might be happening?
> Question 2) Any way to re-initiate the nfs and mountd daemons without
>             re-booting the entire system?
> 
> Thanking you in advance for any assitance you might be able to provide.
> 
> 
> -- 
> John L. Allen
> Director of Information Technology
> 
> GeoNet Technologies Inc.             (902)887-3170
> P.O. Box 3989                        (902)887-2349 fax
> Central Bedeque, PEI                 Email: johna@auracom.com
> C0B 1G0

The directory '/sbin/init.d' on you DEC UNIX machine contains  
startup-scripts for every daemon or service started at boot-time. You'll  
find a script named 'nfs'. To restart your NFS-Server on the AlphaStation  
just type: './nfs stop' and then './nfs start'. (You have to do this as  
root of course).
If you only want to restart the mount-daemon you could do this by sending  
the HUP-signal to it. Type 'kill -HUP `cat /var/run/mountd.pid`'. (Don't  
forget the single back-quotes around the cat-command.)



