matsaki@statcan.ca wrote:
> 
> Has anyone successfully compiled this version of Samba on v4.0d?

Not on 4.0d, but on 4.0a... AlphaServer 2000 4/233. I simply followed
the instructions EXACTLY (UNIX_INSTALL.txt in the docs directory).

> cc: Severe: includes.h, line 125: Cannot find file <stdio.h> specified in
> #include directive.
> 
> I checked inside the includes.h file, and line 125 is commented out like so:
> #include <stdio.h>

In C, that is NOT a comment. The "#" symbol typically precedes all
include directives (some C gurus can correct me or provide more info. I
think different characters mean different things). First, I would check
to make sure you uncommented/commented/corrected the needed/wanted
section(s) of the makefile. The platform section I uncommented was:

	# This is for OSF1 (Alpha) V2.0/V4.0 Enhanced Security 
	# contributed by Udo Linauer <ul@eacpc4.tuwien.ac.at>
	FLAGSM = -DOSF1 -DOSF1_ENH_SEC -DFAST_SHARE_MODES
	LIBSM = -lsecurity

You should only uncomment one platform section. The package does not
automatically determine the platform/variant that is being used. Next, I
would double check your compiler installation. "stdio.h" is a standard
part of most C compilers (Standard Input Output Routines). Have you had
any problems compiling anything else? Is stdio.h living in the
/usr/include directory? Does it have the proper ownership/protections?
(mine is bin:bin with -r--r--r--)

Dunno if this helps any, but it might be a start...

Later...
Ed

