: If you're using AdvFS, vdump is the best way to go for filesystems.
: Alternatively to file backups is GNU tar.

It depends on what you are actually trying to do.
People get confused between dumps, backups and archives.

<soapbox mode on>
A DUMP is a complete bit by bit copy of a file system. It is the *only*
backup that allows you to recover everything on a file system. Hence it
only works at the filesystem level.
Digital Unix has two native file systems: Advanced File System (AdvFS)
and Unix File System (UFS - based on the Berkeley Fast Filesystem).

The vdump(8) command can backup both UFS and AdvFS file systems.
The dump(8) command can only handle UFS.
I would recommend if you want to do a dump use vdump.
Also see vrestore(8) for how to recover a dump.


A BACKUP is a copy of your data. The problem with the term backup is that
it is very loosly applied. You can make a backup of your data (not necessarily
your filesystem) with several programs:

vdump(8)	complete AdvFS or UFS filesystem backup.
		Handles recovery of complete file system.

dump(8)		complete UFS filesystem backup.
		Handles recovery of complete file system.

dd(1)		bit image copy of disk partition, file or other source.
		Handles recovery of image recovery.

tar(1)		Standard Unix tar program. Path names are limited to 80 chars.
		Thus it will blow up with deep directories.
		Does not follow symbolic links unless told to do so.
		Does not backup /dev special devices.

gtar(1)		GNU version of tar. Path names are longer (can't remember
		how long - the source tells all).

cpio(1)		Standard unix cpio program. Similar to tar but path lengths
		are limited to 255 characters (I think).
		Better end of media handling.

NSR		DEC's version of Legato's Networker software.
		This writes data out in a proprietary format on tape
		but does support true DUMPING, archives and recovery.

An ARCHIVE is a copy of your data that has been independently verfied on
a second device, often made into several copies and stuck away in a safe place.
Archives typically are copies of End of Month/Year backup tapes.
Long term archives need to worry about issues like print through,
humidity, periodic read testing and system obselesence.
IMHO anything less is not an ARCHIVE but a backup.

<soapbox mode off>

I hope this helps.

