I've compiled SAMBA on 4.0d with no problem.
Checking the configuration file is a good
idea.  Note that particular section is
for enhanced security configurations.

It is very odd that it can't find the
stdio.h file.

The # symbol, when it's typically the
first character in a line is a
"preprocessor directive."  It tells
the compiler to do something special.

The "#include" tells it to drop the
contents of another file at that
point when compiling.

The <> symbols found around the file name
of "stdio.h" specify that the file should
exist in one of the standard locations that
the compiler searches for header files.

Cut and paste this small piece of code into
a file, say "teststd.c" and see how it works:

#include <stdio.h>

int main( void )
{
  printf( "Test!\n" );

  return( 0 );
}

Compile it using "cc -std1 teststd.c" and
then via "cc teststd.c".  If it fails under
either compilation you may have a compiler
problem.


