Ole Holm Nielsen (Ole.H.Nielsen@fysik.dtu.dk) wrote:
>  It appears that Digital UNIX does not have a way of taking a dump
>  image of a workstation and installing it via the network. The Remote
>  Installation Services (see "man ris) does not seem to offer this
>  functionality.

The functionality offered by RIS is actually much more flexible than
simple cloning a workstation over the network, see the "Dataless Managent
and Remote Installation Server Guide", available at the Digital's web
site. (Reading the ris utility source, which is a rather large shell
script, is also instuctive.) By using selection lists and manually 
preconfiguring some of the files in the installation images it is 
possible to achieve most of the "image cloning" functionality using RIS.

It is a bit of hassle to install and configure, though, so that if you 
have just a dosen workstations it is *much* easier to boot the generic
kernel from the CD (or let the RIS to prepage generic kernel for booting
over the network), manually initialize the network interface and do
the moral equivalent of:

rsh server dd if=/dev/rz16c bs=2048k | dd of=/dev/rz16c bs=2048k

and reboot (vdump and vrestore will work fine, too). Changing the 
network address and host hame is trivial once the clone comes up.

If you have a set of identical workstations functionining mostly as
a farm of compute servers, you might also want to set up them for
remote boot operation. This way you'll save a bit of disk space on
each of the stations (which may be significant with just 4.3G), and
a lot of administration hassle each time you'll need to update your
cluster. The chores are *mostly* covered by the guide mentioned
above, but be prepared to configure the DMS environment by hand and
to tinker a bit with the insides of the /usr/sbin/dmu script.

You better have a fast network and either a separate physical subnet 
or segmenting switch between yourself and the rest of the world
to get consistently good performance, though. The remote boot setup
is also not a good idea if your cluster needs to serve a lot of
interactive users - network latencies and paging code over the
network will kill you. I wish DU had an option of spilling *unmodified*
pages to the local disk instead of discarding them if the pages
come from over the network...

Anyway, we have a cluster of 24 500au-s set up in this way, used
both as a compute server and light development server (with at most
2 users on each of the cluster machines at any given time). We are
quite pleased with the performance (which is CPU-bound 95% of the 
time) and amount of the administration (which is essentially zero)
required to keep it running.

>  Unfortunately, although our 500au workstations come with a PCI SCSI
>  adapter that has an external Wide-SCSI connector, the external SCSI
>  adapter has been disabled !. 

My understanding is that it's not disabled, but simply cannot be used
reliably due to the UltraSCSI cabling rules. The au-series SCSI
controller (which is simply Qlogic QLA1040 card as far as I can tell),
has three SCSI connectors, two of them internally. All three can be
used at the same time, but ONLY if the total length of the cabling
(including drive stubs) attached to all three connectors TOGETHER
does not exceed 4 feet. If you'll examine the SCSI cable supplied 
with your 500au, you'll see that it's already about three foot long,
which precludes the *reliable* use of the external connector at
UltraSCSI speeds. If you use a high-quality (and short!) external
cabling and replace the internal cable with a shorter one, you may 
be able to get it to work.

In any event, all au-series boxes manufactured after March 1st, 1998
can support up to two Qlogic cards, which gives you the external
connector on the second channel. (Keeping your external deviced on
a separate SCSI bus may be a good idea in any event).

Incidentally, I do not think the Adaptec PCI SCSI adapters are supported
by 4.0, which is really a shame - at least here it is very easy (and 
cheap!) to pick up an 2940U, while Qlogic cards are extremely hard to
find.

>  Unless you want to fiddle with installing and removing internal disks
>  or SCSI adapters, you just cannot clone a Digital UNIX workstation :-(

See above.

Regards,

/Serge.P



