Debugging hints:
If you start automount with -T -T it will prevent it from daemonizing and
will print all sorts of useful info.

Look in daemon.log for syslog messages.  Use the -v option for more of them.

>This should mount the network directory
>server.domain.com:/usr/server/home/
>into a/usr/users/server/home/ and make a soft link into
>/usr/users/server/ named
>home -> a/usr/users/server/home/.
>This should be as described in manual!

The only mount that should get done is to intercept accesses to
/usr/users/server - use mount -e to check that.

>But: -the directory is not mounted,

server.domain.com:/usr/server/home/ should not be mounted.

 even if I change to
>a/usr/users/server/home

Then server.domain.com:/usr/server/home/ should be mounted.

>         -the soft link is not generated

That's part of the mount.  (BTW, the softlink only resides in a small
NFS pseudo-server that is part of automount.  Yes, it's confusing!)

>         -something is mounted into /usr/users/server/, then I don't
>have write access
>            to this directory and files, which was in this directory
>cannot be accessed anymore;
>            the directory is empty after this procedure.

Mounting on top of a active directory makes the covered directory and
its contents inaccessible.  This is true of mount physical file systems
too.

Play with the -T stuff and that should help a lot.  BTW, never stop
automount with kill -9, it will badly mess up things.
