Jyrki Havia (havia@cc.helsinki.fi) wrote:

: 	We have Digital Unix 4.0D (with patchset 1) logging
: 	via syslog from remote machines.

: 	Under 4.0B the format of logline was

: Jul  7 13:13:30 <originating hostname> <message...>

: 	Now, under 4.0D the format is

: Jul  7 13:13:30 <the machine itself> <message...>

: 	I.e. the syslogd does not put the remote hostname as the
: 	host of message, instead it replaces it with it's own
: 	hostname.

Are you *quite* sure you are really seeing the *remote* syslog
messaged? The syslogd shipped with the original 4.0D is broken
(or the documentation is broken, it depends on your point of
view) and does not allow any remote logging. In order to enable
remote logging with plain 4.0D, you'll have to create an 
(undocumented) file /etc/syslog.auth. Since its not documented (<g>),
it is hard to say that the intended contents of this file are.
By poking inside the binary and experimenting a bit, I got this
format to work for me:

remote_host_name_1 all
remote_host_name_2 all
etc...

This may have been fixed in patchset 1, but I do not see 
any reference to it in the release notes (I never bothered
to install it - if it aint broke, don't fix it).

Now, if you also want to use remote logging for binlogd, Digital's 
DU wizards managed to break that one in 4.0D, too. The binary
looks for the binlog/udp port in the /etc/services, while the
/etc/services provided with the distribution defines binlogd/udp
instead. Took a bit of hair-pulling to get that one.

Which makes me wonder that sort of the quality assurance and
regression testing DU gets before the release. Probably none...

Regards,

/Serge.P



